description:
Write a class `Fraction` with:

- a constructor `Fraction(int numerator, int denominator)` that uses a **member initializer list**; if the denominator is 0, store 1 instead (a fraction may never have a zero denominator);
- a default constructor, so that `Fraction f{};` is 0/1;
- `int numerator() const` and `int denominator() const`.

starter.solution.cpp:
class Fraction
{
public:

private:
    int m_num{ 0 };
    int m_den{ 1 };
};

solution.solution.cpp:
class Fraction
{
public:
    Fraction() = default;

    Fraction(int numerator, int denominator)
        : m_num{ numerator }, m_den{ denominator == 0 ? 1 : denominator }
    {
    }

    int numerator() const { return m_num; }
    int denominator() const { return m_den; }

private:
    int m_num{ 0 };
    int m_den{ 1 };
};

unitTests:
TEST("two-argument constructor") { const Fraction f{ 3, 4 }; CHECK_EQ(f.numerator(), 3); CHECK_EQ(f.denominator(), 4); }
TEST("default constructor is 0/1") { const Fraction f{}; CHECK_EQ(f.numerator(), 0); CHECK_EQ(f.denominator(), 1); }
TEST("a zero denominator becomes 1") { const Fraction f{ 5, 0 }; CHECK_EQ(f.numerator(), 5); CHECK_EQ(f.denominator(), 1); }

